---
/**
 * Who manufactures Ayurvedic medicine in India, and what their websites permit.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. No public register joins these facts. AYUSH manufacturing licences are
 * issued by state licensing authorities and the registers are state-held, largely offline and not
 * joined to one another; the trade associations publish member lists as PDFs or not at all. So
 * "who makes Ashokarishta" and "which Ayurvedic manufacturers can an answer engine read" are both
 * unanswerable from anything published, and the second one this project can measure exactly.
 *
 * WHY IT IS SHAPED AS A TABLE OF PERMISSIONS RATHER THAN A DIRECTORY. A directory of Indian
 * manufacturers would be a commodity and mostly unverifiable: addresses, licence numbers and
 * product ranges change, and this project has no standing to assert them. What it can assert, from
 * one fetch per host, is what each site itself says about being read. That is a fact with a
 * timestamp and a method, and it answers the question the rest of this site keeps running into.
 *
 * THE RESULT RAN AGAINST THE EXPECTATION. The plan behind this work assumed the opening was
 * largest where companies BLOCK AI crawlers, because then nothing can cite them for their own
 * products. Almost nobody blocks: 152 of 164 allow GPTBot, ClaudeBot and CCBot, and only 4 name an
 * AI crawler at all. The sector is wide open and still absent from answer engines, which means the
 * barrier is not permission. It is that there is nothing on most of these pages worth citing.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import register from '../data/manufacturer-register.json';
import { abs, datasetIdentity } from '../lib/site';

const data = register as any;
const s = data.summary;
const companies = (data.companies as any[]);

const title = 'Who manufactures Ayurvedic medicine in India, and what their sites allow';
const description = `${s.companies} Ayurvedic manufacturers across ${s.states} Indian states, each `
  + `with the website it publishes and what its robots.txt permits, measured one host at a time on `
  + `${data.measuredOn}. ${s.catalogueReadable} publish a catalogue a crawler may read; only `
  + `${s.nameAnyAiCrawler} address an AI crawler at all.`;
const canonical = '/manufacturers/';

const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const AI = ['GPTBot', 'ClaudeBot', 'CCBot', 'PerplexityBot'];

/** By state, then by name, because the state is the one axis a reader navigates this by. */
const byState = new Map<string, any[]>();
for (const c of companies) {
  const k = c.state ?? 'State not established';
  if (!byState.has(k)) byState.set(k, []);
  byState.get(k)!.push(c);
}
const states = [...byState.entries()]
  .map(([state, list]) => ({ state, list: list.slice().sort((a, b) => String(a.name).localeCompare(String(b.name))) }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.list.length - a.list.length || a.state.localeCompare(b.state));

const named = companies.filter((c) => (c.aiAgentsNamed ?? []).length > 0);
const blocks = companies.filter((c) => c.permits && AI.some((a) => c.permits[a] === false));
/**
 * A CONSIDERED decision, which is narrower than "blocks something".
 *
 * `blocks` counts any host refusing any AI crawler, and that set includes one with a blanket
 * `Disallow: /` which refuses Googlebot too. A site closed to everything has not made a decision
 * about AI; it has made no decision about anything. The interesting group is the one that named
 * these crawlers AND refused the training ones AND kept the search engines, because that is a
 * distinction somebody sat down and drew. The page said "two of the four" when the register held
 * 164, then rendered "4 of the 6" from `blocks` when it grew, which is how a count drifts into a
 * claim it was never measuring.
 */
const TRAINING = ['GPTBot', 'ClaudeBot', 'CCBot'];
const considered = named.filter((c) => TRAINING.some((a) => c.permits?.[a] === false)
  && c.permits?.Googlebot === true);
const unreadable = companies.filter((c) => c.robots?.verdict === 'unknown');
const permitCount = (agent: string) => companies.filter((c) => c.permits?.[agent] === true).length;

const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Dataset',
    ...datasetIdentity(),
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#dataset`,
    name: data.name,
    description: data.description,
    url: abs(canonical),
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    creator: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    dateModified: data.measuredOn,
    variableMeasured: ['manufacturer name', 'website host', 'state', 'robots.txt status',
      'robots.txt verdict', 'catalogue readable', 'AI crawlers named', 'per-crawler permission'],
    spatialCoverage: { '@type': 'Place', name: 'India' },
    distribution: [
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'JSON', encodingFormat: 'application/json', contentUrl: abs('/manufacturer-register.json') },
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'CSV', encodingFormat: 'text/csv', contentUrl: abs('/manufacturer-register.csv') },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Page title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} updated={data.measuredOn}
  trail={[{ label: 'Ayurvedic manufacturers in India' }]} jsonld={jsonld}>
  <p class="answer">
    There is no public register of who manufactures Ayurvedic medicine in India. Licences are issued
    by state authorities and held by them, and the trade bodies publish little. This is
    <strong>{n(s.companies)} manufacturers across {n(s.states)} states</strong>, each with the site
    it publishes and what that site permits a crawler to read, measured one host at a time on
    {data.measuredOn}.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    The measurement was made expecting to find companies shutting answer engines out. They are not.
    <strong>{n(permitCount('GPTBot'))} of {n(s.companies)} allow GPTBot, ClaudeBot and CCBot</strong>,
    {n(s.catalogueReadable)} publish a catalogue this crawler may read, and only
    {n(s.nameAnyAiCrawler)} address an AI crawler by name at all. Permission is not the barrier.
  </p>

  <table class="facts">
    <caption>What was measured</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Manufacturers</th><td>{n(s.companies)}, in {n(s.states)} states and union territories</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Selling classical preparations</th><td>{n(s.sellClassicalPreparations)}, by their own description</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">robots.txt with rules</th><td>{n(s.robotsVerdicts.rules ?? 0)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">robots.txt absent or empty</th><td>{n(s.robotsVerdicts.open ?? 0)}, which permits everything</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">robots.txt unreadable</th><td>{n(s.robotsVerdicts.unknown ?? 0)}, treated as closed and left alone</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Catalogue readable by this crawler</th><td>{n(s.catalogueReadable)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Naming any AI crawler</th><td>{n(s.nameAnyAiCrawler)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Blocking at least one</th><td>{n(s.blockAtLeastOneAiCrawler)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Candidates held back</th><td>{n(s.unverifiedCandidatesHeldBack)}, unverified, names not published</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What each crawler is allowed</h2>
  <p>
    One robots.txt per host, parsed per agent, for the path <code>/products/</code>. A host whose
    robots.txt could not be fetched counts as refusing, which is what RFC 9309 requires and what
    this project does everywhere.
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Crawler</th><th>Allowed</th><th>Refused</th><th>Could not be established</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {['Googlebot', 'bingbot', ...AI].map((a) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="term">{a}</td>
            <td>{n(companies.filter((c) => c.permits?.[a] === true).length)}</td>
            <td>{n(companies.filter((c) => c.permits?.[a] === false).length)}</td>
            <td>{n(unreadable.length)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The {named.length} that addressed AI crawlers, and what they decided</h2>
  <p>
    Worth naming individually because there are so few of them, and because {considered.length} of
    the {named.length} drew a distinction the rest of the industry has not: they refuse GPTBot,
    ClaudeBot and CCBot while keeping Googlebot. Counted rather than typed, because this moves every
    time the register grows and a hardcoded "four" was already wrong once.
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Company</th><th>Site</th><th>Googlebot</th><th>GPTBot</th><th>ClaudeBot</th><th>CCBot</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {named.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="term">{c.name}</td>
            <td><code>{c.host}</code></td>
            {['Googlebot', 'GPTBot', 'ClaudeBot', 'CCBot'].map((a) => (
              <td>{c.permits?.[a] === true ? 'allowed' : c.permits?.[a] === false ? 'refused' : 'unknown'}</td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    None of them is a large company. Every manufacturer in this register with a nationally known
    name leaves the question unaddressed, which is the more interesting half of the result: the
    decision about whether an answer engine may read a product catalogue is currently being taken,
    where it is taken at all, by the smallest firms in the industry.
  </p>

  <h2>The register</h2>
  <p>
    By state, then alphabetically. "Classical" means the company describes itself as making
    shastrokta preparations, which is its own claim and not a verification of it. "Catalogue" is
    whether this crawler may read a product path, which is a fact about permission and not about
    whether anything useful is published there.
  </p>
  {states.map(({ state, list }) => (
    <section class="state">
      <h3>{state} <span class="count">{list.length}</span></h3>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Company</th><th>Site</th><th>Classical</th><th>robots.txt</th><th>Catalogue</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {list.map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="term">{c.name}</td>
                <td><code>{c.host}</code></td>
                <td>{c.sellsClassical ? 'yes' : ''}</td>
                <td>{c.robots?.verdict === 'unknown' ? 'unreadable' : c.robots?.verdict}</td>
                <td>{c.catalogueReadable ? 'readable' : 'no'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>How this was built, and what it is not</h2>
  <p>
    Candidates were found by research agents working from association rosters, government
    directories and trade listings. That work is <strong>not trusted</strong>, and the reason is
    concrete: the same passes proposed a gambling site, a parked domain, a betting blog, two wrong
    legal entities and one company already counted under another name.
    {n((data.excluded as any[]).length)} candidates are excluded here with the reason recorded
    against each, and {n(s.unverifiedCandidatesHeldBack)} more are held back entirely with their
    names unpublished, because the verification pass that should have checked them did not complete.
    A list of real companies published on one agent's say-so is exactly the thing this site refuses
    elsewhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every permission field above comes from this project fetching that host's robots.txt once,
    1.2 seconds apart, identified as <code>NighantuBot</code> with a URL explaining itself. No
    product page was fetched for this register. It therefore records what each site says about being
    read and nothing about what it publishes, the quality of anything it makes, or whether it holds
    a current licence. A manufacturer absent from this register is not absent from the industry:
    there are several thousand licensed Ayurvedic manufacturing units in India and this is
    {n(s.companies)} of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/manufacturer-register.json">manufacturer-register.json</a> and
    <a href="/manufacturer-register.csv">manufacturer-register.csv</a>, CC BY 4.0. The CSV is one
    row per company with the per-crawler permissions as columns, which is the shape for anyone
    wanting to count or filter. If a row here is wrong about your company, including the state or
    whether we read your robots.txt correctly, tell us and we will correct it.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">
    Measured {data.measuredOn}. Related: <a href="/choosing/who-publishes-the-composition/">which of
    these companies publish what is in the bottle</a>, and <a
    href="/choosing/label-dose-against-the-formulary/">how their stated doses compare to the
    formulary</a>.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .tablewrap th, .tablewrap td { text-align: left; padding: .4rem .7rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: .93rem; }
  .tablewrap td.term { font-weight: 600; }
  .tablewrap code { font-size: .86rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .state { margin: 1.6rem 0; }
  .state h3 { margin: 0 0 .3rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .state h3 .count { font-weight: 400; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
